text:
Casts
cast() converts columns to other types. Conversions follow R’s as.*() functions. A value that cannot be converted becomes NA, and phibble raises one E_USER_WARNING per column, as R does with “NAs introduced by coercion”.

Floats convert to int by truncating toward zero.
"TRUE", "true", "T", and "True" convert to true. "yes" and "1" do not.
Floats convert to text with up to 15 significant digits, in fixed or scientific notation, whichever is shorter.

set_error_handler(function (int $level, string $message): bool {
    echo "Warning: {$message}\n";

    return true;
});

$df = DataFrame::fromColumns([
    'x' =&gt; [2.7, -2.7, '3.9', 'abc'],
    'flag' =&gt; ['TRUE', 'T', 'yes', '0'],
    'n' =&gt; [1.0, 0.1 + 0.2, 1e5, 1 / 3],
]);

$cast = $df-&gt;cast(['x' =&gt; 'int', 'flag' =&gt; 'bool', 'n' =&gt; 'string']);

echo $cast;
Warning: NAs introduced by coercion to int in column 'x' (1 value).
Warning: NAs introduced by coercion to bool in column 'flag' (2 values).
# A tibble: 4 × 3
      x flag  n                
  &lt;int&gt; &lt;lgl&gt; &lt;chr&gt;            
1     2 TRUE  1                
2    -2 TRUE  0.3              
3     3 NA    1e+05            
4    NA NA    0.333333333333333
A factor converts to int or float as its 1-based level codes, as as.integer(factor) does in R. Converting it to text gives its labels. Dates convert to numbers as days since 1970-01-01, and datetimes as seconds since the epoch.

List-columns
A list-column holds any PHP value in each row, such as arrays or other frames. nest() collapses columns into a list-column of frames, one row per unique combination of the remaining columns. unnest() expands it again.
$penguins = DataFrame::fromCsv('penguins.csv')-&gt;select(['species', 'island', 'body_mass']);

$nested = $penguins-&gt;nest(['island', 'body_mass'], into: 'data');
echo $nested, "\n";

$sizes = $nested-&gt;mutate(['n' =&gt; fn ($r) =&gt; $r-&gt;data-&gt;nrow()]);
echo $sizes-&gt;select(['species', 'n']), "\n";

echo $nested-&gt;unnest('data')-&gt;head(3);
# A tibble: 3 × 2
  species   data              
  &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;list&gt;            
1 Adelie    &lt;tibble [152 × 2]&gt;
2 Gentoo    &lt;tibble [124 × 2]&gt;
3 Chinstrap &lt;tibble [68 × 2]&gt; 
# A tibble: 3 × 2
  species       n
  &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;int&gt;
1 Adelie      152
2 Gentoo      124
3 Chinstrap    68
# A tibble: 3 × 3
  species island    body_mass
  &lt;chr&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;         &lt;int&gt;
1 Adelie  Torgersen      3750
2 Adelie  Torgersen      3800
3 Adelie  Torgersen      3250

Model data
toModelData() returns columns in the form phit’s model functions read. Numbers, bools, and strings are plain lists. Dates and datetimes become days and seconds since the epoch. A factor becomes an array with its levels, its 0-based codes, and whether it is ordered, so a model keeps the frame’s level order, including unused levels. A list-column throws.
$df = tibble(
    mass: [3750.0, 3800.0, null],
    male: [true, false, true],
    species: ['Adelie', 'Gentoo', 'Adelie'],
)-&gt;asFactor('species', ['Adelie', 'Chinstrap', 'Gentoo']);

echo json_encode($df-&gt;toModelData(), JSON_PRETTY_PRINT), "\n";
{
    "mass": [
        3750,
        3800,
        null
    ],
    "male": [
        true,
        false,
        true
    ],
    "species": {
        "levels": [
            "Adelie",
            "Chinstrap",
            "Gentoo"
        ],
        "codes": [
            0,
            2,
            0
        ],
        "ordered": false
    }
}

Building expressions
An expression has four kinds of nodes:

Col reads a column: col('price').
Lit holds a literal value: lit(3), lit('north'), a date, or a list of values for in.
Call applies a registered function to arguments.
Desc marks a sort key or ranking as descending.

Methods on any expression build operator calls. Arguments that are not expressions are wrapped in lit().







Method
Operator




add, sub, mul, div, mod, pow
+, -, *, /, %%, ^


neg
unary minus


eq, ne, lt, le, gt, ge
==, !=, &lt;, &lt;=, &gt;, &gt;=


and, or, not
&, |, !


isNa()
is.na()


isIn([...])
%in%


between($low, $high)
between()


desc()
desc()



Functions in the Phibble\Expr namespace build the other calls. Options are named arguments, as in mean(col('x'), na_rm: true) or lag(col('x'), n: 2, default: 0). var_() stands for var(), which is a reserved word in PHP. call('name', ...) builds a call to any registered function by name.
columns() lists the columns an expression reads, and toArray() returns the tree as plain arrays:
$heavy = col('body_mass')-&gt;gt(5000)-&gt;and(col('species')-&gt;isIn(['Gentoo', 'Chinstrap']));

echo json_encode($heavy-&gt;toArray(), JSON_PRETTY_PRINT), "\n";
echo json_encode($heavy-&gt;columns()), "\n";
{
    "type": "call",
    "fn": "&",
    "args": [
        {
            "type": "call",
            "fn": "&gt;",
            "args": [
                {
                    "type": "col",
                    "name": "body_mass"
                },
                {
                    "type": "lit",
                    "value": 5000
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "call",
            "fn": "in",
            "args": [
                {
                    "type": "col",
                    "name": "species"
                },
                {
                    "type": "lit",
                    "value": [
                        "Gentoo",
                        "Chinstrap"
                    ]
                }
            ]
        }
    ]
}
["body_mass","species"]

Serialization
toArray() returns a JSON-friendly array, and Expr::fromArray() rebuilds the expression. Each node has a type key: col, lit, call, or desc. A call lists its positional arguments under args and its options under named. A date literal carries dtype (date or datetime).
$expr = lag(col('score'), n: 2, default: 0, order_by: col('week'));
$json = json_encode($expr-&gt;toArray());

echo $json, "\n";

$back = Phibble\Expr\Expr::fromArray(json_decode($json, true));
var_dump($back == $expr);
{"type":"call","fn":"lag","args":[{"type":"col","name":"score"}],"named":{"n":{"type":"lit","value":2},"default":{"type":"lit","value":0},"order_by":{"type":"col","name":"week"}}}
bool(true)
The test suite checks the in-memory evaluator against dplyr. The script tests/r/expr.R evaluates the same expressions in R and stores the results in tests/fixtures/expr/expected.json, which other backends can reuse.

The examples on this site import the frame class and phibble’s functions like this:
use Phibble\DataFrame;
use function Phibble\{tibble, tribble};
use function Phibble\Expr\{col, desc, mean, n};
Phibble\Expr has functions named min, max, sum, round, abs, log, floor, str_pad, str_split, str_replace, and a few others that share names with PHP built-ins. In a file that imports them, a bare round($x) calls phibble’s expression helper. Write \round($x) for PHP’s function.

The examples on this site import the frame class and phibble’s functions like this:
use Phibble\DataFrame;
use function Phibble\{tibble, tribble};
use function Phibble\Expr\{col, desc, mean, n};
Phibble\Expr has functions named min, max, sum, round, abs, log, floor, str_pad, str_split, str_replace, and a few others that share names with PHP built-ins. In a file that imports them, a bare round($x) calls phibble’s expression helper. Write \round($x) for PHP’s function.

Building frames
tibble() builds a frame column by column. Each named argument is a column. A list gives one value per row, and a single value is repeated for every row. A closure receives the frame built so far, so a column can be computed from earlier ones.
$scores = tibble(
    student: ['ana', 'ben', 'cy'],
    score: [81.5, 74.0, null],
    passed: fn (DataFrame $t) =&gt; array_map(fn ($s) =&gt; $s === null ? null : $s &gt;= 75, $t['score']),
    term: 'fall',
);

echo $scores;
# A tibble: 3 × 4
  student score passed term 
  &lt;chr&gt;   &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;lgl&gt;  &lt;chr&gt;
1 ana      81.5 TRUE   fall 
2 ben      74   FALSE  fall 
3 cy       NA   NA     fall
tribble() takes the column names first and then one list per row, which is easier to read for small literal tables:
$sites = tribble(
    ['site', 'lat', 'opened'],
    ['north', 44.9, 2019],
    ['south', 41.2, 2021],
);

echo $sites;
# A tibble: 2 × 3
  site    lat opened
  &lt;chr&gt; &lt;dbl&gt;  &lt;int&gt;
1 north  44.9   2019
2 south  41.2   2021
DataFrame::fromRows() takes a list of associative arrays. Keys missing from a row are NA. DataFrame::fromColumns() takes an array of columns and accepts explicit types.
$rows = DataFrame::fromRows([
    ['id' =&gt; 1, 'dose' =&gt; 2.5, 'arm' =&gt; 'a'],
    ['id' =&gt; 2, 'arm' =&gt; 'b'],
]);
$cols = DataFrame::fromColumns(['id' =&gt; [1, 2], 'arm' =&gt; ['a', 'b']], types: ['arm' =&gt; 'factor']);

echo $rows, "\n\n", $cols;
# A tibble: 2 × 3
     id  dose arm  
  &lt;int&gt; &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;chr&gt;
1     1   2.5 a    
2     2  NA   b    

# A tibble: 2 × 2
     id arm  
  &lt;int&gt; &lt;fct&gt;
1     1 a    
2     2 b
DataFrame::fromCsv() reads a CSV file and guesses each column’s type. The input and output guide covers its options.

Reading values
A column reads as a PHP list with $df-&gt;name, $df['name'], or $df-&gt;column('name'). Rows are Phibble\Row objects, which read values as properties or array keys. Iterating a frame yields its rows. An unknown column name throws Phibble\Exception\ColumnNotFound; names are never matched partially.
$df = tibble(x: [1, 2, 3], y: ['a', 'b', 'c']);

var_dump($df-&gt;x === $df['x']);
echo json_encode($df-&gt;column('y')), "\n";

$row = $df-&gt;row(1);
echo $row-&gt;x, ' ', $row['y'], "\n";

foreach ($df as $i =&gt; $row) {
    echo "{$i}: {$row-&gt;y}\n";
}

try {
    $df-&gt;z;
} catch (Phibble\Exception\ColumnNotFound $e) {
    echo $e-&gt;getMessage(), "\n";
}
bool(true)
["a","b","c"]
2 b
0: a
1: b
2: c
Column 'z' not found. Available columns: x, y.

Distinct rows and joins
distinct() keeps the first row of each combination of the given columns. join() takes the other table, the key columns, and the join type: inner, left, right, full, semi, anti, or cross. Joins follow dplyr 1.1, including its column naming and the rule that NA keys match each other.
$penguins = DataFrame::fromCsv('penguins.csv');
$islands = tribble(
    ['island', 'area_km2'],
    ['Biscoe', 6.1],
    ['Dream', 0.9],
);

$joined = $penguins
    -&gt;distinct(['species', 'island'])
    -&gt;join($islands, ['island'], 'left');

echo $joined;
# A tibble: 5 × 3
  species   island    area_km2
  &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;chr&gt;        &lt;dbl&gt;
1 Adelie    Torgersen     NA  
2 Adelie    Biscoe         6.1
3 Adelie    Dream          0.9
4 Gentoo    Biscoe         6.1
5 Chinstrap Dream          0.9
Besides equality keys, a join condition can compare columns, as dplyr’s join_by() does. [$left, $op, $right] with &gt;=, &gt;, &lt;=, or &lt; keeps the pairs that satisfy the comparison, and all conditions must hold. One ['closest', $left, $op, $right] condition keeps, for each row of x, only the y rows with the nearest value: the largest for &gt;= and &gt;, the smallest for &lt;= and &lt;. dplyr’s between(), within(), and overlaps() are pairs of comparisons. In a rolling join, the columns used in comparisons are kept from both tables:
// Each order gets the latest price set on or before its date.
$orders = tribble(
    ['order', 'item', 'day'],
    [1, 'tea', 3],
    [2, 'tea', 9],
    [3, 'coffee', 4],
);
$prices = tribble(
    ['item', 'from_day', 'price'],
    ['tea', 1, 2.5],
    ['tea', 5, 2.75],
    ['coffee', 1, 4.0],
    ['coffee', 6, 4.5],
);

echo $orders-&gt;join($prices, ['item', ['closest', 'day', '&gt;=', 'from_day']], 'left');
# A tibble: 3 × 5
  order item     day from_day price
  &lt;int&gt; &lt;chr&gt;  &lt;int&gt;    &lt;int&gt; &lt;dbl&gt;
1     1 tea        3        1  2.5 
2     2 tea        9        5  2.75
3     3 coffee     4        1  4

Immutability
Methods never change the frame they are called on:
$df = tibble(x: [1, 2, 3]);
$doubled = $df-&gt;mutate(['x' =&gt; col('x')-&gt;mul(2)]);

echo json_encode($df-&gt;x), ' ', json_encode($doubled-&gt;x), "\n";
[1,2,3] [2,4,6]

Method chains and the pipe operator
Every verb returns a new table, so verbs chain as methods on any PHP version phibble supports. PHP 8.5’s |&gt; passes a value to a callable, and arrow functions let the same pipeline use it. phplyr provides verbs that return such callables, so a phplyr pipeline needs no arrow functions.

Method chain (PHP 8.3+)Pipe (PHP 8.5)


$penguins = DataFrame::fromCsv('penguins.csv');

$counts = $penguins
    -&gt;groupBy(['island'])
    -&gt;summarise(['n' =&gt; n()])
    -&gt;arrange([desc(col('n'))]);

echo $counts;


$penguins = DataFrame::fromCsv('penguins.csv');

$counts = $penguins
    |&gt; (fn (DataFrame $f) =&gt; $f-&gt;groupBy(['island']))
    |&gt; (fn (DataFrame $f) =&gt; $f-&gt;summarise(['n' =&gt; n()]))
    |&gt; (fn (DataFrame $f) =&gt; $f-&gt;arrange([desc(col('n'))]));

echo $counts;



# A tibble: 3 × 2
  island        n
  &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;int&gt;
1 Biscoe      168
2 Dream       124
3 Torgersen    52

Guessing
Without explicit types, phibble guesses each column from up to guessMax rows, sampled the way readr 2 samples them. A column is:

bool if every value is T, F, TRUE, FALSE, True, False, true, or false,
int if every value is a whole number (readr guesses double here),
float if every value is a number, including Inf and NaN,
date if every value is an ISO 8601 date,
datetime if every value is an ISO 8601 date and time,
string otherwise.

Numbers with leading zeros such as 007 stay strings, as in readr.
When a value cannot be parsed as its column’s type, or a row has the wrong number of fields, phibble still returns the data. The value becomes NA, and the read raises one E_USER_WARNING that lists the problems.
set_error_handler(function (int $level, string $message): bool {
    echo "Warning: {$message}\n";

    return true;
});

$df = DataFrame::fromCsvString("id,score\n1,2.5\n2,oops\n3\n", types: ['score' =&gt; 'double']);

echo $df;
Warning: One or more parsing issues: row 4: expected 2 columns, got 1; column 'score': 1 value could not be parsed as float.
# A tibble: 3 × 2
     id score
  &lt;int&gt; &lt;dbl&gt;
1     1   2.5
2     2  NA  
3     3  NA

JSON
toJson() writes a list of records, and toJson('columns') writes an object of columns. NA is written as null and kept in every record. DataFrame::fromJson() reads either shape and detects which one it has.
$df = tibble(id: [1, 2], score: [2.5, null]);

echo $df-&gt;toJson(), "\n";
echo $df-&gt;toJson('columns'), "\n";

echo DataFrame::fromJson('{"id": [1, 2], "ok": [true, null]}');
[{"id":1,"score":2.5},{"id":2,"score":null}]
{"id":[1,2],"score":[2.5,null]}
# A tibble: 2 × 2
     id ok   
  &lt;int&gt; &lt;lgl&gt;
1     1 TRUE 
2     2 NA

Databases
DataFrame::fromPdo() runs a query with positional or named bindings and loads the result. Drivers that return numbers as text, as MySQL’s does, are handled by parsing all-text columns with the CSV guessing rules.
$pdo = new PDO('sqlite::memory:');
$pdo-&gt;exec('CREATE TABLE visits (id INTEGER, clinic TEXT, bp REAL, seen TEXT)');
$pdo-&gt;exec("INSERT INTO visits VALUES (1, 'north', 121.5, '2024-05-01'), (2, 'south', NULL, '2024-05-02'), (3, 'north', 133.0, '2024-05-02')");

$df = DataFrame::fromPdo($pdo, 'SELECT * FROM visits WHERE clinic = ?', ['north']);

echo $df;
# A tibble: 2 × 4
     id clinic    bp seen      
  &lt;int&gt; &lt;chr&gt;  &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;date&gt;    
1     1 north   122. 2024-05-01
2     3 north   133  2024-05-02
DataFrame::fromQuery() loads a query builder without depending on its library:

A Laravel query builder, Eloquent builder, or relation is read through toSql(), getBindings(), and its connection’s PDO object.
A Doctrine DBAL QueryBuilder is read through getSQL() and getParameters(). Pass a PDO or DBAL connection as the second argument to run the SQL through PDO. Without one, the builder runs the query itself.

To keep a query in the database and run verbs there, use a SQL table instead.

Laravel collections
DataFrame::fromCollection() reads any object with an all() or toArray() method, such as a Laravel Collection. The items may be arrays, objects, or Eloquent models. toCollection() returns an Illuminate\Support\Collection of row arrays and throws when Laravel is not installed.
// Any object with all() or toArray() works, such as a Laravel Collection
// of arrays or Eloquent models. This stand-in avoids a Laravel dependency.
$collection = new class
{
    public function all(): array
    {
        return [
            ['id' =&gt; 1, 'name' =&gt; 'Ana'],
            (object) ['id' =&gt; 2, 'name' =&gt; 'Ben'],
        ];
    }
};

echo DataFrame::fromCollection($collection);
# A tibble: 2 × 2
     id name 
  &lt;int&gt; &lt;chr&gt;
1     1 Ana  
2     2 Ben

Opening a table
tbl($pdo, 'name') opens a table or view. tbl($pdo, 'SELECT ...', $bindings) starts from a query with positional (?) or named (:name) bindings. The same tables can be opened with SqlTable::table() and SqlTable::query(). SqlTable::fromQuery($builder) starts from a Laravel or Doctrine query builder, and SqlTable::fromSource($pdoSource) from a PdoSource.
phibble reads the column names when the table is opened. It runs the query itself when the rows are needed: collect() returns a DataFrame, and toRows(), nrow(), iteration, and printing also run it.
showQuery() returns the SQL with ? placeholders, and bindings() returns their values in order.
// Load penguins.csv into an in-memory SQLite database.
$pdo = new PDO('sqlite::memory:');
$pdo-&gt;exec('CREATE TABLE penguins (species TEXT, island TEXT, bill_len REAL, bill_dep REAL,
    flipper_len INTEGER, body_mass INTEGER, sex TEXT, year INTEGER)');
$insert = $pdo-&gt;prepare('INSERT INTO penguins VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)');
foreach (DataFrame::fromCsv('penguins.csv')-&gt;toRows() as $row) {
    $insert-&gt;execute(array_values($row));
}

$penguins = tbl($pdo, 'penguins');

$summary = $penguins
    -&gt;filter(col('year')-&gt;eq(2008))
    -&gt;groupBy(['species'])
    -&gt;summarise(['n' =&gt; n(), 'mass' =&gt; mean(col('body_mass'), na_rm: true)]);

echo get_class($summary), "\n\n";
echo $summary-&gt;showQuery(), "\n\n";
echo json_encode($summary-&gt;bindings()), "\n\n";
echo $summary-&gt;collect();
Phibble\Sql\SqlTable

SELECT "species", "n", "mass" FROM (SELECT "species", "n", "mass", ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY ("species" IS NULL), "species") AS "__phibble_row" FROM (SELECT "species" AS "species", COUNT(*) AS "n", (CASE WHEN COUNT("body_mass") = 0 THEN NULL ELSE AVG(CAST("body_mass" AS REAL)) END) AS "mass" FROM (SELECT * FROM (SELECT *, rowid AS "__phibble_row" FROM "penguins") AS q1 WHERE ("year" = ?)) AS q2 GROUP BY "species") AS q3) AS q4 ORDER BY ("__phibble_row" IS NULL), "__phibble_row"

[2008]

# A tibble: 3 × 3
  species       n  mass
  &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;int&gt; &lt;dbl&gt;
1 Adelie       50 3742 
2 Chinstrap    18 3800 
3 Gentoo       46 5020.
Each verb wraps the previous query in a subquery, so each step’s SQL stays simple. Database planners usually flatten the nesting.

Joins
join() between two tables on the same PDO connection compiles to a SQL join. The output columns follow DataFrame::join(): x’s columns, then y’s other columns, with .x and .y suffixes on clashing names. NA keys match each other, as in dplyr, through IS in SQLite, IS NOT DISTINCT FROM in PostgreSQL, and &lt;=&gt; in MySQL. Pass naMatches: false for SQL’s usual equality. The order of rows from a SQL join is not defined, so call arrange() when it matters.
$pdo = new PDO('sqlite::memory:');
$visits = tbl($pdo, "SELECT 1 AS clinic_id, 120 AS bp UNION ALL SELECT 2, 135 UNION ALL SELECT NULL, 128");
$clinics = tbl($pdo, "SELECT 1 AS id, 'north' AS name UNION ALL SELECT NULL, 'unassigned'");

$joined = $visits-&gt;join($clinics, [['clinic_id', 'id']], 'left');

echo $joined-&gt;showQuery(), "\n\n";
echo $joined-&gt;arrange(['bp'])-&gt;collect();
SELECT "clinic_id", "bp", "name" FROM (SELECT jq1x."clinic_id" AS "clinic_id", jq1x."bp" AS "bp", jq1y."name" AS "name", jq1x."__phibble_row" AS "__phibble_row", jq1y."__phibble_row" AS "__phibble_row_1" FROM (SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER () AS "__phibble_row" FROM (SELECT 1 AS clinic_id, 120 AS bp UNION ALL SELECT 2, 135 UNION ALL SELECT NULL, 128) AS phibble_source) AS jq1x LEFT JOIN (SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER () AS "__phibble_row" FROM (SELECT 1 AS id, 'north' AS name UNION ALL SELECT NULL, 'unassigned') AS phibble_source) AS jq1y ON (jq1x."clinic_id" IS jq1y."id")) AS q2 ORDER BY ("__phibble_row" IS NULL), "__phibble_row", ("__phibble_row_1" IS NULL), "__phibble_row_1"

# A tibble: 3 × 3
  clinic_id    bp name      
      &lt;int&gt; &lt;int&gt; &lt;chr&gt;     
1         1   120 north     
2        NA   128 unassigned
3         2   135 &lt;NA&gt;

Dialects
Dialect::for($pdo) chooses the dialect from the PDO driver. The dialects differ in identifier quoting, cast types, string functions, and NULL-safe equality. The SQL for one expression in each dialect:
use Phibble\Sql\Compiler;
use Phibble\Sql\MysqlDialect;
use Phibble\Sql\PostgresDialect;
use Phibble\Sql\SqliteDialect;

$expressions = [
    'mean, NA aware' =&gt; mean(col('bp')),
    'ratio' =&gt; col('bp')-&gt;div(col('baseline')),
    'contains' =&gt; str_detect(col('name'), 'north'),
    'in, with NA' =&gt; col('arm')-&gt;isIn(['a', null]),
];

foreach ([new SqliteDialect(), new PostgresDialect(), new MysqlDialect()] as $dialect) {
    echo "-- {$dialect-&gt;name()}\n";
    $compiler = new Compiler($dialect, mode: 'summary');
    foreach ($expressions as $label =&gt; $expr) {
        echo \str_pad($label, 16), $compiler-&gt;compile($expr)-&gt;sql, "\n";
    }
    echo "\n";
}

$window = new Compiler(new PostgresDialect(), groups: ['clinic'], order: [[col('visit'), false]]);
echo $window-&gt;compile(cumsum(col('bp')))-&gt;sql, "\n";
-- SQLite
mean, NA aware  (CASE WHEN COUNT(*) &gt; COUNT("bp") THEN NULL ELSE (CASE WHEN COUNT("bp") = 0 THEN NULL ELSE AVG(CAST("bp" AS REAL)) END) END)
ratio           (CASE WHEN "baseline" = 0 THEN (CASE WHEN "bp" &gt; 0 THEN 9e999 WHEN "bp" &lt; 0 THEN -9e999 WHEN "bp" = 0 THEN NULL END) ELSE CAST("bp" AS REAL) / "baseline" END)
contains        (INSTR("name", ?) &gt; 0)
in, with NA     (COALESCE("arm" IN (?), FALSE) OR "arm" IS NULL)

-- PostgreSQL
mean, NA aware  (CASE WHEN COUNT(*) &gt; COUNT("bp") THEN NULL ELSE (CASE WHEN COUNT("bp") = 0 THEN 'NaN'::float8 ELSE AVG(CAST("bp" AS DOUBLE PRECISION)) END) END)
ratio           (CASE WHEN "baseline" = 0 THEN (CASE WHEN "bp" &gt; 0 THEN 'Infinity'::float8 WHEN "bp" &lt; 0 THEN '-Infinity'::float8 WHEN "bp" = 0 THEN 'NaN'::float8 END) ELSE CAST("bp" AS DOUBLE PRECISION) / "baseline" END)
contains        (POSITION(? IN "name") &gt; 0)
in, with NA     (COALESCE("arm" IN (?), FALSE) OR "arm" IS NULL)

-- MySQL
mean, NA aware  (CASE WHEN COUNT(*) &gt; COUNT(`bp`) THEN NULL ELSE (CASE WHEN COUNT(`bp`) = 0 THEN NULL ELSE AVG(CAST(`bp` AS DOUBLE)) END) END)
ratio           (CASE WHEN `baseline` = 0 THEN (CASE WHEN `bp` &gt; 0 THEN NULL WHEN `bp` &lt; 0 THEN NULL WHEN `bp` = 0 THEN NULL END) ELSE CAST(`bp` AS DOUBLE) / `baseline` END)
contains        (LOCATE(?, `name`) &gt; 0)
in, with NA     (COALESCE(`arm` IN (?), FALSE) OR `arm` IS NULL)

(CASE WHEN COUNT(*) OVER (PARTITION BY "clinic" ORDER BY ("visit" IS NULL), "visit" ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) &gt; COUNT("bp") OVER (PARTITION BY "clinic" ORDER BY ("visit" IS NULL), "visit" ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) THEN NULL ELSE SUM("bp") OVER (PARTITION BY "clinic" ORDER BY ("visit" IS NULL), "visit" ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) END)
The test suite runs the dplyr reference cases through SqlTable on SQLite, and on PostgreSQL and MySQL when their DSNs are set. See Development.
